    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_1157c27c8c86b4999a28f159e00a7087c391ebf4c07dde1744a3db36ee3a32a8",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "A finite verification can be exported as a compact bounded predicate—such as RH through height H or RH for a stated modulus-height family of Dirichlet L-functions—so a later analytic argument can consume the certified range without treating the underlying zero list as the theorem. The downstream effect depends on the consumer and may be a slight constant improvement, a rigorously delimited input to an unconditional proof, or a threshold-triggered bound.",
