    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_cfa921b02753f6ce2727502a91a50521e52be79454ee7714e74c1bbef57b0486",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The sources use three non-equivalent normalizations to ask progressively broader questions about close zeros: division by local mean spacing makes one observed gap comparable across height; the product of squared gap and inverse-square interaction tests the pair against its full zero environment; and a convex potential of reference-normalized gaps aggregates deviations to support a population-level contradiction with positive-density small gaps.",
