    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_fb05848109edaeca5dba2814a3f6bf108844a2dd031836f9a2f248eba0f375a5",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Both analyses view heat-deformed zeros through inverse-square interaction, but they compress the environment at different scales. The close-pair method subtracts two zero velocities to obtain a gap ODE with a singular pair term and a scalar environmental correction; the many-body method localizes to finite subsystems, exposes cross-boundary terms, subtracts a reference lattice through a convex defect, and justifies an asymptotic dissipation identity for a localized Hamiltonian.",
